1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,933
Your CTO asks: "We have 340
branches on the server.

2
00:00:07,973 --> 00:00:11,405
Which ones can be deleted
without losing anything?"

3
00:00:11,745 --> 00:00:16,756
To answer, you have to know what would
be deleted. If a branch is a copy of the

4
00:00:16,796 --> 00:00:21,870
code, deleting one deletes code. If a
branch is the set of commits that branched

5
00:00:21,910 --> 00:00:27,175
off, deleting one deletes commits. A commit,
remember, is one saved snapshot of the

6
00:00:27,215 --> 00:00:31,586
project. So which is it: a copy, a
set of commits, or something else?

7
00:00:31,626 --> 00:00:32,609
Say your answer.

8
00:00:37,449 --> 00:00:42,737
Something else. The polls in the course's
research report found that 58 percent of

9
00:00:42,777 --> 00:00:47,935
practising developers think of a branch
as the commits that branched off, and 15

10
00:00:47,975 --> 00:00:53,132
percent as a pointer. The second group is
right, in a way that decides outcomes.

11
00:00:53,172 --> 00:00:57,161
A branch is one line of text: a
name and the ID of one commit.

12
00:00:57,201 --> 00:01:00,929
Everything in the next five
videos follows from that fact.

13
00:01:00,969 --> 00:01:04,697
And the 340 branches? We come
back to them before the end.

14
00:01:06,537 --> 00:01:10,373
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:10,413 --> 00:01:15,455
This block of five videos is about refs:
branches, HEAD, tags, and the relations

16
00:01:15,495 --> 00:01:20,728
between them. A ref is a name for an object
ID. The textbook opens the chapter with

17
00:01:20,768 --> 00:01:26,000
three CTO questions: the 340 branches, a
contractor whose two days of work vanished

18
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:31,082
after she checked out a tag, and every
laptop failing with cannot lock ref since

19
00:01:31,122 --> 00:01:36,355
this morning. All three are questions about
refs, and none has a Git command as its

20
00:01:36,395 --> 00:01:36,800
answer.

21
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:40,505
Today we establish the three
facts under all of it.

22
00:01:40,545 --> 00:01:46,114
A branch is a ref. HEAD is a symbolic ref.
And a commit writes one ref: the one that

23
00:01:46,154 --> 00:01:46,848
HEAD names.

24
00:01:48,688 --> 00:01:50,266
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:50,606 --> 00:01:52,019
After this video you can:

26
00:01:52,359 --> 00:01:57,123
Define a branch in one sentence without
"copy", and prove the sentence from .git.

27
00:01:57,463 --> 00:02:02,004
Create, read and delete a branch with
plumbing, and compare with porcelain.

28
00:02:02,344 --> 00:02:06,562
Describe the three contents of .git/HEAD:
on a branch, detached, unborn.

29
00:02:06,902 --> 00:02:09,042
Trace the two ref writes of one commit.

30
00:02:10,882 --> 00:02:16,001
A branch is a ref. In one sentence: a branch
is a ref, a name under refs/heads/ that

31
00:02:16,041 --> 00:02:20,976
holds the ID of one commit, the tip. The
branch's history is whatever that commit

32
00:02:21,016 --> 00:02:22,695
reaches through its parents.

33
00:02:23,035 --> 00:02:28,035
Precisely, from the glossary: a branch head
is "a named reference to the commit at

34
00:02:28,075 --> 00:02:33,013
the tip of a branch", stored "in a file
in $GIT_DIR/refs/heads/ directory, except

35
00:02:33,053 --> 00:02:36,577
when using packed refs". The
ref holds nothing but the ID.

36
00:02:36,617 --> 00:02:41,739
There's no list of the branch's commits, no
record of where it started, no owner, no

37
00:02:41,779 --> 00:02:46,472
creation date. git log feature is a walk
that starts at the ID in the ref and

38
00:02:46,512 --> 00:02:47,762
follows parent lines.

39
00:02:48,102 --> 00:02:55,453
Inside .git. With the files backend, which
Git 2.55 uses by default, a loose branch

40
00:02:55,493 --> 00:03:01,508
is the file .git/refs/heads/<name>,
containing the ID and a newline.

41
00:03:01,548 --> 00:03:07,652
Packed refs move the same information
into lines of .git/packed-refs.

42
00:03:07,692 --> 00:03:14,597
The reftable backend keeps refs in a
binary store. The reflog of a branch, the

43
00:03:14,637 --> 00:03:20,830
journal of where it has pointed,
lives in .git/logs/refs/heads/<name>.

44
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:27,774
That's why plumbing is the reliable reader.
Plumbing means Git's low-level commands,

45
00:03:27,814 --> 00:03:32,202
made for scripts, and porcelain
means the everyday ones.

46
00:03:32,242 --> 00:03:38,766
A script that reads .git/refs/heads/main
breaks on the first packed repository, and

47
00:03:38,806 --> 00:03:41,060
on every reftable repository.

48
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:44,980
Creating a branch by plumbing.
git update-ref writes a ref.

49
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:50,072
It is 🟡 CAUTION. It makes two checks that
porcelain also relies on: the object must

50
00:03:50,112 --> 00:03:53,385
exist, and a ref under
refs/heads/ must name a commit.

51
00:03:53,425 --> 00:03:57,925
Writing the file by hand bypasses both
checks, the locking and the reflog.

52
00:03:57,965 --> 00:04:01,299
The textbook labels hand-written
ref files 🔴 DANGEROUS.

53
00:04:01,639 --> 00:04:06,589
git update-ref -d deletes a ref together
with its reflog, with no merge check, and

54
00:04:06,629 --> 00:04:10,849
is 🔴 DANGEROUS. What it can destroy
is the only name of those commits.

55
00:04:10,889 --> 00:04:14,561
The preview is git rev-parse on
the ref, writing the ID down.

56
00:04:14,601 --> 00:04:19,612
The recovery is to recreate the ref from
that ID. And it's appropriate for refs you

57
00:04:19,652 --> 00:04:24,541
created yourself, as in today's demo. Twig
looks worried, and with a red label on

58
00:04:24,581 --> 00:04:25,636
screen, fairly so.

59
00:04:25,976 --> 00:04:31,360
HEAD is a symbolic ref. In one sentence:
HEAD is the ref that says where you are:

60
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:36,582
normally a symbolic ref that names the
current branch, and in detached state a

61
00:04:36,622 --> 00:04:38,524
direct reference to a commit.

62
00:04:38,864 --> 00:04:44,212
A symbolic ref is, in the manual's words,
"a regular file that stores a string that

63
00:04:44,252 --> 00:04:49,665
begins with ref: refs/". When HEAD contains
ref: refs/heads/main, every command that

64
00:04:49,705 --> 00:04:54,988
needs "the current commit" resolves HEAD
to refs/heads/main, and then to the ID in

65
00:04:55,028 --> 00:05:00,246
that ref. HEAD is per worktree, so each
working directory of a repository has its

66
00:05:00,286 --> 00:05:00,506
own.

67
00:05:00,846 --> 00:05:05,431
So .git/HEAD has three possible contents.
On a branch: the name of a ref that

68
00:05:05,471 --> 00:05:10,417
exists. Detached: a commit ID. Unborn: the
name of a ref that doesn't exist yet, as

69
00:05:10,457 --> 00:05:13,060
in a new repository
before the first commit.

70
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:19,605
What a commit does. In one sentence: a
commit writes the new commit's ID into the

71
00:05:19,645 --> 00:05:25,003
ref that HEAD names, and nothing else
moves. .git/HEAD keeps its text.

72
00:05:25,043 --> 00:05:31,325
Other branches stay where they are. Two
reflogs gain a line each: the branch's and

73
00:05:31,365 --> 00:05:34,872
HEAD's. Those are the
two ref writes to trace.

74
00:05:40,212 --> 00:05:44,567
When this matters. Because a branch is
a name for a commit, "the branch" has a

75
00:05:44,607 --> 00:05:48,907
different value in every repository that
holds a copy of it: your clone, each

76
00:05:48,947 --> 00:05:53,528
colleague's clone, the server. Watch: a
teammate pushes a commit, and the server's

77
00:05:53,568 --> 00:05:58,206
main moves while yours stays put. main on
the server and main in your clone are two

78
00:05:58,246 --> 00:06:02,827
refs that happen to share a name. A statement
such as "the fix is on main" is only

79
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:04,968
meaningful with a repository attached.

80
00:06:06,808 --> 00:06:11,545
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
a branch is a bookmark in a book that is

81
00:06:11,585 --> 00:06:16,439
still being written. The bookmark marks one
page. The chapters before that page are

82
00:06:16,479 --> 00:06:20,508
"the story so far", but they're the
book's pages, not the bookmark's.

83
00:06:20,548 --> 00:06:25,285
Moving the bookmark changes nothing in the
book, and two bookmarks can sit on the

84
00:06:25,325 --> 00:06:25,874
same page.

85
00:06:26,214 --> 00:06:31,549
The analogy breaks at the edge: pages
after a bookmark exist too, unless nothing

86
00:06:31,589 --> 00:06:35,580
points at them, and then Git
may eventually throw them away.

87
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:40,188
Add HEAD to the picture: a slip of paper on
your desk that names the bookmark you're

88
00:06:40,228 --> 00:06:43,521
using. When you write a new page,
you move that bookmark forward.

89
00:06:43,561 --> 00:06:45,932
The slip on the desk still
says the same thing.

90
00:06:47,772 --> 00:06:49,834
Now the same bookmarks, as files.

91
00:06:50,174 --> 00:06:56,609
Three refs, three small files, one history.
Two of the refs name the same commit.

92
00:06:56,649 --> 00:07:02,525
Creating the second and third branch
wrote no commit, no tree and no blob.

93
00:07:02,865 --> 00:07:06,856
Before: two branch labels on one commit,
HEAD attached to the feature label.

94
00:07:07,196 --> 00:07:11,693
After: one new commit. The feature
label moved to it, and main stayed.

95
00:07:13,533 --> 00:07:17,830
Create a branch with porcelain. git
branch <name> 🟢 SAFE: it adds a ref.

96
00:07:18,170 --> 00:07:22,667
Predict what is in the file
.git/refs/heads/feature/retry-backoff.

97
00:07:22,707 --> 00:07:23,767
Say it out loud.

98
00:07:28,607 --> 00:07:33,198
The full ID of 7aecf06, and nothing else.
git for-each-ref is the plumbing listing:

99
00:07:33,238 --> 00:07:37,328
for every ref, the ID, the type of
object it names, and the full ref name.

100
00:07:37,368 --> 00:07:41,904
Both branches name the same commit. The
branch also received a reflog, whose first

101
00:07:41,944 --> 00:07:46,479
line records how it was created. That's
the proof for the one-sentence definition:

102
00:07:46,519 --> 00:07:50,107
two commands, cat and git rev-parse,
print the same forty digits.

103
00:07:50,447 --> 00:07:52,459
Now plumbing. git update-ref 🟡 CAUTION.

104
00:07:52,799 --> 00:07:57,309
A third branch, created without git branch.
And the two checks: an ID that names no

105
00:07:57,349 --> 00:08:00,597
object is refused, and so is an
object that is not a commit.

106
00:08:00,937 --> 00:08:06,020
Writing the file by hand works, and is the
thing not to do: git branch -v reads the

107
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:10,403
hand-written file like any other, but
git reflog show by-hand is empty.

108
00:08:10,443 --> 00:08:13,612
No Git command wrote this
ref, so nothing logged it.

109
00:08:13,652 --> 00:08:18,735
git update-ref -d 🔴 DANGEROUS deletes it.
The five questions were answered a moment

110
00:08:18,775 --> 00:08:21,883
ago, and this ref is one
the script created itself.

111
00:08:22,223 --> 00:08:27,655
Now the reason to read refs with
plumbing and never by path.

112
00:08:27,695 --> 00:08:34,131
Predict what cat .git/refs/heads/main
prints after git pack-refs --all.

113
00:08:34,171 --> 00:08:36,594
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

114
00:08:41,434 --> 00:08:45,348
An error. The loose file is gone,
and the ref lives in packed-refs.

115
00:08:45,388 --> 00:08:48,830
git rev-parse main doesn't care:
the branch still resolves.

116
00:08:51,670 --> 00:08:54,415
Four ways to read HEAD,
from raw to friendly.

117
00:08:54,755 --> 00:09:00,894
A new repository: HEAD points at refs/heads/main,
but no such ref exists until the

118
00:09:00,934 --> 00:09:07,224
first commit writes it. git rev-parse --verify
HEAD fails, git branch lists nothing,

119
00:09:07,264 --> 00:09:13,478
and git branch feature fails, because there's
no commit to point the new branch at.

120
00:09:13,818 --> 00:09:17,107
Now move HEAD alone, with git
symbolic-ref HEAD <ref> 🟡 CAUTION.

121
00:09:17,147 --> 00:09:19,916
It rewrites one file. Predict
what git status reports.

122
00:09:20,256 --> 00:09:25,239
The index and the working tree still hold
the content of main, so git status reports

123
00:09:25,279 --> 00:09:30,263
the difference between the commit HEAD now
names and the index as a staged deletion.

124
00:09:30,303 --> 00:09:35,047
The index is the list of what goes into
the next commit, and the working tree is

125
00:09:35,087 --> 00:09:38,755
your files on disk. Switching
back makes the report disappear.

126
00:09:38,795 --> 00:09:43,778
A branch switch is this ref change plus an
update of the index and the working tree.

127
00:09:43,818 --> 00:09:45,094
That's the next video.

128
00:09:47,934 --> 00:09:53,340
Two branches on the same commit,
23b0907, and HEAD names the new one.

129
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:59,891
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. Write
three lines: the text of .git/HEAD, the

130
00:09:59,931 --> 00:10:06,285
ID in the feature ref, and the ID in main.
Next to each, write whether one commit

131
00:10:06,325 --> 00:10:10,073
will change it. Pause me,
and write your answer.

132
00:10:18,913 --> 00:10:24,531
One of the three changed. .git/HEAD is
unchanged. feature/retry-backoff moved to

133
00:10:24,571 --> 00:10:30,259
f5192c8, and main stayed on 23b0907. The
branch reflog gained a commit: line, and

134
00:10:30,299 --> 00:10:35,845
the HEAD reflog shows the switch and the
commit. If you expected HEAD's text to

135
00:10:35,885 --> 00:10:38,037
change, you're in good company.

136
00:10:39,877 --> 00:10:41,525
Five mistakes to watch for.

137
00:10:41,865 --> 00:10:45,141
Describing a branch as a
copy or as a set of commits.

138
00:10:45,181 --> 00:10:50,146
Root cause: the ref holds one commit ID
and nothing else; the history is reached

139
00:10:50,186 --> 00:10:51,460
through parent links.

140
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:57,864
Reading .git/refs/heads/<name> in a script.
Root cause: refs may be packed or stored

141
00:10:57,904 --> 00:11:01,570
in reftable; use git rev-parse
or git for-each-ref.

142
00:11:01,910 --> 00:11:07,443
Writing a ref file by hand. Root cause: it
skips validation, locking and the reflog;

143
00:11:07,483 --> 00:11:08,704
use git update-ref.

144
00:11:09,044 --> 00:11:13,772
git branch feature fails in a new repository.
Root cause: the current branch is

145
00:11:13,812 --> 00:11:17,213
unborn, so there is no commit
to point the new branch at.

146
00:11:17,553 --> 00:11:20,764
Saying "the fix is on main"
without naming a repository.

147
00:11:20,804 --> 00:11:25,525
Root cause: main is a different ref with
possibly a different value in every clone

148
00:11:25,565 --> 00:11:26,570
and on the server.

149
00:11:28,410 --> 00:11:34,410
Now, out of the lab. "I committed to the
wrong branch" is, as the textbook puts it,

150
00:11:34,450 --> 00:11:39,504
a ref problem and not a content
problem. The commit object is correct.

151
00:11:39,544 --> 00:11:45,179
Only the name that moved is wrong. An
engineer on an evaluation team makes two

152
00:11:45,219 --> 00:11:50,564
commits on main that belong on a feature
branch. Nothing has to be copied.

153
00:11:50,604 --> 00:11:56,531
The repair is two ref writes: create a
branch at the current commit, and move main

154
00:11:56,571 --> 00:11:58,860
back. Video 26 runs that repair.

155
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:03,095
And the CTO's 340 branches?
Deleting a branch deletes a name.

156
00:12:03,135 --> 00:12:07,999
Whether anything is lost depends on
whether another name still reaches those

157
00:12:08,039 --> 00:12:13,289
commits. That's a reachability question,
and video 26 answers it with three tests.

158
00:12:15,129 --> 00:12:22,718
Your turn. Do Lab 4.1, "Refs by hand",
in lab-manual/m04-refs-branches-head.md.

159
00:12:23,058 --> 00:12:32,118
Before each plumbing command, predict which
file under .git will change, and whether

160
00:12:32,158 --> 00:12:40,784
a reflog line will be written. Before the
failure scenario, predict what git log

161
00:12:40,824 --> 00:12:44,467
--all will say about a broken ref.

162
00:12:46,307 --> 00:12:52,589
Q93: "What exactly is a branch? Define
it in one sentence that mentions neither

163
00:12:52,629 --> 00:12:58,831
"copy" nor "line of development", and
prove the definition with two commands."

164
00:13:01,171 --> 00:13:06,594
Answer out loud. A strong answer gives the
sentence without hesitation, says what

165
00:13:06,634 --> 00:13:12,124
the ref doesn't contain, and chooses two
commands whose outputs can be compared on

166
00:13:12,164 --> 00:13:17,115
the spot. Add why you wouldn't prove
it by reading a file under .git/refs.

167
00:13:18,955 --> 00:13:23,603
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say, in your own words: a branch is a ref

168
00:13:23,643 --> 00:13:28,012
under refs/heads/ that holds the ID of
one commit, and its history is what that

169
00:13:28,052 --> 00:13:32,142
commit reaches. Creating a branch writes
one small ref and copies nothing.

170
00:13:32,182 --> 00:13:36,495
Refs can be loose files, packed, or in
reftable, so I read them with plumbing.

171
00:13:36,835 --> 00:13:40,709
HEAD is a symbolic ref that
names the current branch.

172
00:13:40,749 --> 00:13:45,657
It can also hold a commit ID, or
name a branch that isn't born yet.

173
00:13:45,697 --> 00:13:51,859
A commit writes the new ID into the ref that
HEAD names, appends to two reflogs, and

174
00:13:51,899 --> 00:13:53,706
leaves HEAD's text alone.

175
00:13:55,546 --> 00:13:58,980
Read sections 7.1 to 7.4 of Chapter 7.

176
00:13:59,320 --> 00:14:06,064
Do Exercise 4.1, Level 1, "a branch, and
the file behind each step", and Exercise

177
00:14:06,104 --> 00:14:11,928
4.2, Level 1, "HEAD follows you", in
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

178
00:14:12,268 --> 00:14:17,739
Challenge: Exercise 4.6, Level 2, "from
which branch was it created?", in the same

179
00:14:17,779 --> 00:14:18,076
file.

180
00:14:18,416 --> 00:14:22,931
Today you proved from the dot git folder
that a branch is one line of text.

181
00:14:22,971 --> 00:14:26,757
Create and delete one by hand in
the lab before the next video.

182
00:14:26,797 --> 00:14:31,737
Next time: git branch and git switch. Until
then, look at the state first and type

183
00:14:31,777 --> 00:14:33,681
second. See you in the next one.
